{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfbrnCt5i6P2MNpLNqopk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 04 is done: the repaired rules cost almost nothing, and they never gave a different answer from the published rules in any run. Every finished refutation of optimum − 1 came back `unsat` under both settings, so no certified value moved. In the pathwidth solver, all 731 graphs proved under both settings got the same width. Each of those widths also matches the recorded bench results.\n\n**Extra nodes visited by the repaired rules** (pairs where both settings finished):\n\n| Where | Pairs finished | Extra nodes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Corpus, up to 40 customers | 6,135 of 6,135 | +0.004% |\n| Chu & Stuckey, 50–100 customers (600 s per call) | 118 of 125 | +0.30% (worst class `100-50-4` at +1.0%) |\n| 125 × 125 (cap of 2 × 10⁸ nodes) | 11 of 23 | −0.01% |\n| Pathwidth `solve` (VSPLIB, colouring, named, 500 Rome graphs) | 731 of 880 proved | +2.0%, mostly Rome (median pair 1.000, worst 1.13) |\n\n**Time per node:** about +2% in the MOSP C (from −0.1% to +5.8% over six hard instances, measured with repeated runs under a fixed node cap). About +6% in the pathwidth descents, but that was measured on a loaded machine.\n\n**How often the old test passes but the matching fails:**\n- **Definite move:** 0.07–0.13% of candidates at 9–100 customers, 0.27% at 125 × 125 (up to the node cap), and 0.02% on the pathwidth graphs.\n- **Better move:** 0.02–0.06% of candidate pairs.\n- **Nodes where the definite move no longer fires at all:** 0.007–0.08%. Usually a later candidate fires instead, which is why this is lower than the failure rate.\n\n**Gaps:**\n- **The hardest refutations weren't measured for tree size.** `Random-100-100-2` and the 125 × 125 density-2 and density-4 instances hit the budget under both settings. For them the report gives only the per-node cost and how often each rule fires.\n- **One graph is excluded.** `DorogovtsevGoltsevMendesGraph` (3,282 vertices) ignores its time budget; the recorded bench run took 4,007 s. I stopped that worker pool by PID after 1.8 hours.\n\n**Code changes (measurement only, the search is unchanged):**\n- `customer_search.c` now counts how often each rule fires, readable through a new entry point `cs_last_rule_counts`. `closing_search.c` is again a byte copy of it.\n- `satisfiability/native.py` exposes the counts as `last_rule_counts()`.\n- The counters don't slow anything down: compared with the C from before this item, the published rules run 1–5% faster per node.\n- A new test checks that reading the counters leaves node counts unchanged and that the counts are consistent.\n\nThe gate passes (1,344 MOSP tests, 110 pathwidth tests). Nothing was written to `solutions/`. The results are a new \"Item 04\" section in `paper2/solver_fix.md`, with the scripts in `paper2/solver_fix_cost.py` and `paper2/solver_fix_cost_tables.py`. The data are in `paper2/data/solver_fix_cost_*.csv` and the tables in `paper2/data/solver_fix_cost_tables.md`. PROGRESS.md now reads 4/10, and item 04 is marked `[x]` in iterations.md."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":702,"cache_read_input_tokens":176241,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":702,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":815,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"25acc2df-f9dd-4945-9455-4c38c02b4b56","uuid":"f1692610-2960-4986-b1fc-cd4a756a605a","timestamp":"2026-10-01T16:48:36.361Z","request_id":"req_011CfbrnCRoUMgk75MrQHF7g"}
